WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.560
Glycolysis has two distinct phases.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.060
In the investment phase, two ATP are consumed to phosphorylate glucose.

3
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.220
In the payoff phase, four ATP are produced.

4
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.520
Two from phosphoglycerate kinase and two from pyruvate kinase.

5
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:22.660
Subtract the investment from the payoff and the net yield is two ATP per glucose,

6
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.740
plus two NADH. Aerobic respiration then extracts roughly 30 more, which is why

7
00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.780
oxygen-dependent metabolism is so much more efficient.

8
00:00:32.883 --> 00:00:36.943
The central problem fermentation solves is a redox bookkeeping problem.

9
00:00:37.663 --> 00:00:43.143
Glycolysis consumes NAD+, +, and produces NADH at the glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate

10
00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:46.603
step. But the cell has only a finite NAD+ +, pool.

11
00:00:47.523 --> 00:00:53.943
Fermentation regenerates NAD+ +, by passing electrons from NADH to pyruvate, making

12
00:00:53.943 --> 00:00:58.023
lactate, or to acetaldehyde, made from pyruvate, making ethanol.

13
00:00:58.023 --> 00:01:04.563
The two cycles are tightly coupled, consume NAD+ +, here, regenerate it there, so

14
00:01:04.563 --> 00:01:06.203
that glycolysis can keep running.

15
00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:08.747
Here's a useful metaphor.

16
00:01:09.307 --> 00:01:11.087
Think of a metabolic pathway as a pipe.

17
00:01:11.607 --> 00:01:14.527
A reversible reaction is like a valve you can partially close.

18
00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:16.887
Pressure equilibrates on both sides.

19
00:01:17.387 --> 00:01:19.727
An irreversible reaction is a one-way valve.

20
00:01:20.287 --> 00:01:22.047
Close it and flow stops completely.

21
00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:27.107
Cells mainly regulate metabolism at irreversible steps, because those can be

22
00:01:27.107 --> 00:01:30.107
throttled. Control is still shared with other steps.

23
00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:33.667
Block any essential step and the whole line shuts down.

24
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:40.360
Glycolysis is regulated at three enzymes, hexokinase, phosphofructokinase-1, and

25
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:43.800
pyruvate kinase. Each responds to different energy signals.

26
00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.460
Hexokinases 1 to 3 are inhibited by their product, glucose-6-phosphate.

27
00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:54.840
PFK-1 is the master throttle, activated by a MP in fructose 2, 6 -bisphosphate,

28
00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:56.840
inhibited by ATP and citrate.

29
00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:02.740
Liver pyruvate kinase is activated feed-forward by fructose-1,6-bisphosphate.

30
00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:07.760
Together, these three checkpoints match glycolytic flux to the cell's energy needs.

31
00:02:08.783 --> 00:02:11.743
Think of phosphofructokinase-1 as a turnstile.

32
00:02:12.403 --> 00:02:17.103
Glucose passes through it at step 3, which helps the cell regulate carbon flow into

33
00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:20.883
glycolysis. In the liver, fructose sneaks in downstream.

34
00:02:21.663 --> 00:02:27.243
It bypasses this gate and enters as DHAP and glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate, though

35
00:02:27.243 --> 00:02:28.323
other controls remain.

36
00:02:29.003 --> 00:02:31.683
A large fructose load bypasses this checkpoint.
